 "stimulus_html": "<figure>\n<img src=\"exercises/media/3afa9b4fed40db1f.png\" alt=\"The pamphlet is called A Patriot’s Creed. Each statement in the creed is followed by the origin of the phrase. The creed reads: “I believe in the United States of America (from the Preamble to the Constitution). As a government of the people, by the people, for the people (from the Gettysburg Speech, Abraham Lincoln). Whose just powers are derived from the consent of the governed (From the Declaration of Independence). A democracy in a republic (from <i>The Federalist, Number 10;</i> Article 10, Constitution of the US). A sovereign nation of many sovereign States (Article 4, Constitution of the US, from “E Pluribus Unum,” motto of the Great Seal of US). A perfect Union (from Preamble of Constitution). One and Inseparable (From Speech of Webster, in US Senate 1-26-1830). Established upon those principles of freedom, equality, justice and humanity, for which American patriots sacrificed their lives and fortunes (From Declaration of Independence). I therefore believe it is my duty to my country to love it (From Edward Everett Hale’s <i>The Man Without a Country</i>). To support its Constitution (From Oath of Allegiance, Section 1757, Rev. Stats. US). To obey its laws (From Washington’s Farewell Address; Article 6, Constitution of US). To respect its flag (The Star-Spangled Banner; Army and Navy Regulations, War Department, Flag Etiquette, April 14, 1917). And to defend it against all enemies. Amen (From Oath of Allegiance, Section 1757, Rev. Stats. US).” The bottom of the pamphlet says “To uphold my Creed I have subscribed to War Stamps” and there is a place to sign.\">A 1918 pamphlet.\n</figure>\n<p><i>Refer to the image provided. </i></p>",
